<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sancho García of Castile</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1017</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-lara">House of Lara</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sancho García was count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> from 995 to 1017. He fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> and his sons and took advantage of the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, intervening in its civil war in 1009 and recovering frontier fortresses. He granted generous fueros to frontier settlers, earning the nickname 'of the good laws', and his daughter's marriage linked Castile to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sancho was the son of Count García Fernández and Ava of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a>, daughter of Raymond I, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallars">Pallars</a> and Ribagorza. With the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> he rebelled against his father, and the county was divided between them until his father's death five years later, when Sancho became count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and Álava.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Sancho then turned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almanzor">Almanzor</a>, together with García Gómez and their cousin García Sánchez II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>. He led the coalition defeated at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cervera">Cervera</a> in July 1000, but early that September he turned back the Córdoban invasion of his county. Almanzor's death in 1002 left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">Caliphate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> in crisis. In 1010 Sancho intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a>, ending Muslim domination there; his aunt Countess Toda abdicated and the county was divided between William Isarn, who had been educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, and Raymond Sunyer of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pallars">Pallars</a>, husband of Sancho's sister Mayor.</p><p>His name el de los Buenos Fueros comes from the rights and charters he granted to various towns. In 1011 he founded the Monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> de Oña for his daughter Tigridia to direct as abbess.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sancho died on 5 February 1017 and was buried at Oña. He married Urraca, who is identified, from the name the couple gave a daughter, as a sister of García Gómez and daughter of Count Gómez Díaz of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saldana">Saldaña</a>. Their son García, born in November 1009, succeeded him. Their daughter Sancha was betrothed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 1016 to Berenguer Ramon I, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a>, and married him by 1021, and the eldest, Muniadona, married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Pamplona</a>, through whom the right to the county eventually passed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sancho García of Castile” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Sancho García</b> granted fueros to frontier settlers, and the counts fought Almanzor and his successors.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-castile">County of Castile</a></li></ul></section>
